title: The Exchange
start: start

== start ==
bg booth_desk
music room_tone
The exchange is one room with two walls of it, and both walls are sockets.
Forty lines to the north side of town, thirty to the south, and a board of
little brass shutters that drop when somebody lifts a receiver somewhere
out there in the dark.
You have worked it for eleven years. On Friday it is being replaced.
The card on the desk says SHIFT: 22:00 - 06:00. Under it, in your own hand,
the same thing it says every night: *logged*.
-> first_call

== first_call ==
A shutter drops. Line 12.
You take the plug, you take the jack, you put one in and one in, and you say
the thing you say.
you: Number, please.
voice_12: It's only me. I wanted to hear somebody say it.
You know the voice. You are not supposed to say that you know it.
* "Go ahead, caller." -> put_through
* Say nothing and wait. -> wait_a_moment
* "It's late, Margaret." -> name_her

== wait_a_moment ==
You wait. The line hums, which is not silence but is the closest the exchange
gets to it. On the board, the brass shutter for line 12 stays down.
voice_12: All right. All right.
-> put_through

== name_her ==
you: It's late, Margaret.
voice_12: You're not meant to know my voice.
you: Eleven years.
voice_12: Then you'll know why I'm ringing.
~ knows_margaret true
-> put_through

== put_through ==
you: Go ahead, caller.
You put her through to the number she gives, which is her own house, and
which has been her own house for forty years, and which she is ringing from.
The line clicks. The shutter rises. You write it in the log, because that is
the job: time, line, duration, and nothing else.
The log is a ruled book and it is the only record that the night happened.
-> the_pattern

== the_pattern ==
Three more calls, all ordinary, all logged.
At 01:20 a call comes in on line 29. The south board.
The number she asks for is 0041.
There is no 0041. There has never been a 0041. The north side runs to 0040
and the south side starts at 0042, and that gap is not a number, it is
where the two boards are wired apart.
you: I'm sorry, caller. That number's not on this exchange.
voice_29: It was on Tuesday.
* "Margaret, was that you on twelve?" -> margaret_yes [if knows_margaret]
* "It was on Tuesday." -> check_the_log

== margaret_yes ==
you: Margaret, was that you on twelve?
voice_29: It was.
you: And this is you as well.
voice_29: There's only been the one of me on this board, and after Friday
there won't be any of me on it. I rang the number that isn't there because
somebody ought to have used it before they take the board down.
~ told_the_truth true
-> check_the_log

== check_the_log ==
You look at Tuesday. You keep the logs in a stack and you have kept them
because nobody has ever asked you to throw them away.
Tuesday, 01:20, line 29, and in the duration column, in your own hand: 0041.
You do not remember writing it. You remember every number you have written
down for eleven years, and you do not remember this one.
-> tuesday_call

== tuesday_call ==
The Tuesday log says the call lasted four minutes.
Four minutes is a long time to connect somebody to nothing.
There is one other thing in the margin of that page, in pencil, which is not
something you use, and it says: DO NOT REOPEN.
* Ask the caller who they are. -> ask_who
* Ring 0041 yourself. -> ring_it
* Close the log and go back to the board. -> close_it

== ask_who ==
you: Who am I speaking to?
voice_29: You're the last one on this board. I'm the first one on the next.
you: There isn't a next.
voice_29: There's a room in the building across the yard with no plugs in it
and a machine that does this in a quarter of a second, and on Friday you
are going to teach it what a number is.
~ told_the_truth true
-> friday

== ring_it ==
You take a plug and you put it in a socket that is not there.
Nothing happens, which is what should happen, and then the shutter for line 29
drops again, by itself, with no receiver lifted anywhere in the town.
voice_29: That's the one. Hold it.
-> friday

== close_it ==
You close the log. You go back to the board. There are four hours left and
you work them, and eleven calls come in and go out, and every one of them
goes where it says it goes.
At 05:40 you open the log to Tuesday again. The margin is blank.
-> friday

== friday ==
The room across the yard has a desk and a chair and a terminal, and the
terminal has one instruction on it, which is to describe, in your own words,
what a number is for.
You sit down. You have until six.
-> ending

== ending ==
bg office_night
music fan_hum
You write it out. Not the wiring and not the boards, because the machine has
those. What you write is that a number is a way of agreeing to meet, and that
somebody has to be at the other end, and that when you put a plug in a socket
you are promising that the other end is a person.
You do not mention 0041. You have thought about it since Tuesday and you have
decided it is not a fault in the exchange. It is a gap in the numbering, and
gaps are where the two boards are wired apart, and the machine will have one
too.
* Hand over the log and say nothing else. -> ending
* Tell him about 0041. -> ending_told [if told_the_truth]

== ending_told ==
At six the day man comes in and you hand him the log, and then you tell him
about 0041, and about Tuesday, and about the pencil, and he listens the way
people listen to a man on his last shift, which is politely.
He says he will put it in the notes.
The machine across the yard will have the notes, and the notes will have 0041
in them, and the gap in the numbering will be a line in a file, which is
what happens to gaps now.
You go home. The board goes on Friday. Somebody, somewhere, is still ringing
0041, and there is nobody at the other end, and there is nobody at this one
either.
-> END
